// Title: Ps.exe Renamed SysInternals Tool
// Sigma ID: 18da1007-3f26-470f-875d-f77faf1cab31
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: Florian Roth (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2017-10-22 | Modified: 2023-05-02
// ATT&CK: stealth, G0035, T1036.003
// Tags: car.2013-05-009, detection.emerging-threats
// Description: Detects renamed SysInternals tool execution with a binary named ps.exe as used by Dragonfly APT
//   group and documented in TA17-293A report
// False positives:
//   - Renamed SysInternals tool
// References:
//   - https://www.us-cert.gov/ncas/alerts/TA17-293A
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/ccbb0369507f796db0b0ae6854c8a2be471b9d84/rules-emerging-threats/2017/TA/Dragonfly/proc_creation_win_apt_ta17_293a_ps.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where ProcessCommandLine contains "ps.exe -accepteula" and ProcessCommandLine contains "-s cmd /c netstat"
